\title{The GL(1) Langlands Correspondence over $\Q$:\\
Cyclotomic Class Field Theory, Self-Duality,\\ and the Quadratic Stratum}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 25, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We present a complete and self-contained development of the abelian --- i.e.\ $\mathrm{GL}(1)$ --- case of the Langlands correspondence over the rational numbers $\Q$, in its sharpest classical incarnation: the cyclotomic case of global class field theory. For each modulus $n$ and each field $L$ that is an $n$-th cyclotomic extension of $\Q$ (so $L \cong \Q(\zeta_n)$), we identify two character groups. On the \emph{automorphic} side stand the Dirichlet characters mod $n$, the finite-order Hecke characters of conductor dividing $n$. On the \emph{Galois} side stand the one-dimensional complex representations of the Galois group $G = \Gal(L/\Q)$. The bridge is the Artin reciprocity isomorphism $G \cong (\Z/n\Z)^\times$, which holds unconditionally over $\Q$ because the cyclotomic polynomial $\Phi_n$ is irreducible. Our main contributions are: (1) the GL(1) correspondence as an explicit group isomorphism; (2) a self-duality theorem exhibiting a (non-canonical) isomorphism $\dual{G} \cong G$; (3) cyclicity of the prime-conductor Galois group, equivalent to the existence of primitive roots; and (4) a complete analysis of the quadratic stratum, showing exactly two quadratic characters on each side at an odd prime. Cardinality identities are obtained structurally, by transport across genuine isomorphisms.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The Langlands program and its ground floor}
The Langlands program relates two superficially unrelated kinds of object: \emph{Galois representations}, encoding the symmetries of solutions to polynomial equations, and \emph{automorphic forms}, highly symmetric analytic objects on arithmetic quotients of Lie groups. The connecting tissue is a family of \emph{reciprocity laws}. The program is organized by the rank of $\mathrm{GL}(d)$. The case $d=2$ already contains the modularity theorem and Fermat's Last Theorem; the general case is largely conjectural.

The case $d=1$, the $\mathrm{GL}(1)$ correspondence, is completely proved and coincides with \emph{class field theory}. In the abelian world ``Galois representations'' are one-dimensional homomorphisms into $\C^\times$, and ``automorphic forms'' are Hecke characters. This paper develops the $\mathrm{GL}(1)$ correspondence over $\Q$ in the cyclotomic family, where every object is finite and explicitly computable.

\subsection{Why the cyclotomic case}
By the Kronecker--Weber theorem every finite abelian extension of $\Q$ lies in some cyclotomic field $\Q(\zeta_n)$; the cyclotomic fields are the universal carriers of abelian class field theory over $\Q$. In this family the Artin reciprocity map becomes the canonical isomorphism $\Gal(\Q(\zeta_n)/\Q) \cong (\Z/n\Z)^\times$, and the correspondence holds \emph{unconditionally for every $n$}, because $\Phi_n$ is irreducible over $\Q$.

\subsection{Summary of results}
Fix $n \ge 1$ and a field $L \cong \Q(\zeta_n)$, with $G = \Gal(L/\Q)$. We establish: Artin reciprocity $G \cong (\Z/n\Z)^\times$; the GL(1) correspondence between Dirichlet characters and one-dimensional Galois representations; self-duality $\dual{G} \cong G$; the counts $\#G = \varphi(n)$, $\#\dual{G} = \#G$, and $\#G = p-1$ for primes; cyclicity at primes; the quadratic stratum count $2$; and local--global factorization for coprime moduli.

\section{Definitions and setting}

Throughout, $n$ is a positive integer, $L$ a field with an $n$-th cyclotomic extension structure over $\Q$, and $G = \Gal(L/\Q)$ the group of $\Q$-algebra automorphisms of $L$.

\begin{definition}[Cyclotomic extension]
$L$ is an \emph{$n$-th cyclotomic extension of $\Q$} if $L$ is generated over $\Q$ by a primitive $n$-th root of unity $\zeta_n$. The canonical example is $L = \Q(\zeta_n) = \Q[X]/(\Phi_n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Units mod $n$]
$(\Z/n\Z)^\times$ consists of residues $a \bmod n$ with $\gcd(a,n)=1$, under multiplication. Its order is Euler's totient $\varphi(n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Dirichlet character]
A \emph{Dirichlet character mod $n$} valued in $\C$ is a multiplicative homomorphism $\chi\colon \Z/n\Z \to \C$ supported on the units; equivalently a homomorphism $(\Z/n\Z)^\times \to \C^\times$. These are the finite-order Hecke characters of conductor dividing $n$ for $\Q$. We write $\mathrm{Dir}(\C, n)$ for the group of all of them under pointwise multiplication.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[One-dimensional Galois representation]
A \emph{one-dimensional complex representation} of $G$ is a homomorphism $\rho\colon G \to \C^\times$. The set $\Hom(G, \C^\times) = \dual{G}$, under pointwise multiplication, is the Galois side of the correspondence.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Character group / Pontryagin dual]
For a finite commutative group $A$, the \emph{character group} is $\dual{A} = \Hom(A, \C^\times)$; for finite $A$ one has $\#\dual{A} = \#A$.
\end{definition}

\section{Artin reciprocity in the cyclotomic case}

\begin{theorem}[Artin reciprocity, cyclotomic; \texttt{artinIso}]\label{thm:artin}
There is a group isomorphism $\artin \colon \Gal(L/\Q) \xrightarrow{\ \cong\ } (\Z/n\Z)^\times$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each $\sigma \in G$ satisfies $\sigma(\zeta_n) = \zeta_n^{a(\sigma)}$ for a unique $a(\sigma) \in (\Z/n\Z)^\times$. The map $\sigma \mapsto a(\sigma)$ is a homomorphism since $(\sigma\tau)(\zeta_n) = \zeta_n^{a(\sigma)a(\tau)}$, injective since $\zeta_n$ generates $L$, and surjective since $[L:\Q] = \deg\Phi_n = \varphi(n) = \#(\Z/n\Z)^\times$. Well-definedness and surjectivity rest on the irreducibility of $\Phi_n$ over $\Q$, which is unconditional.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Abelianness; \texttt{galois\_abelian}]
$G$ is abelian.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The target $(\Z/n\Z)^\times$ is commutative and $\artin$ is injective, so $\artin(\sigma\tau)=\artin(\tau\sigma)$ forces $\sigma\tau=\tau\sigma$.
\end{proof}

\section{The GL(1) correspondence}

\begin{lemma}[Functoriality of the dual; \texttt{precompMulEquiv}]\label{lem:precomp}
For a group isomorphism $e\colon G \xrightarrow{\cong} H$ and commutative $M$, precomposition is an isomorphism $\Hom(H,M) \xrightarrow{\cong} \Hom(G,M)$, $\varphi \mapsto \varphi \circ e$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The inverse is $\psi \mapsto \psi \circ e^{-1}$; both round trips are identities, and $(\varphi_1\varphi_2)\circ e = (\varphi_1\circ e)(\varphi_2\circ e)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[GL(1) Langlands correspondence; \texttt{langlandsGL1}]\label{thm:gl1}
There is a group isomorphism
\[
\mathrm{langlandsGL1}\colon \mathrm{Dir}(\C, n) \xrightarrow{\ \cong\ } \Hom(G, \C^\times), \qquad \chi \mapsto \chi \circ \artin.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Compose the identification of a Dirichlet character with its unit-group homomorphism (\texttt{mulEquivToUnitHom}) with $\mathrm{precompMulEquiv}(\artin)$ from Lemma~\ref{lem:precomp}. Concretely $\chi$ becomes $\sigma \mapsto \chi(\artin(\sigma))$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Counting Dirichlet characters; \texttt{card\_dirichlet\_eq\_totient}]
$\#\,\mathrm{Dir}(\C,n) = \varphi(n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For a finite abelian group $A$ with values in $\C$ one has $\#\Hom(A,\C^\times)=\#A$ since $\C$ has enough roots of unity; here $A=(\Z/n\Z)^\times$ has order $\varphi(n)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Counting Galois representations; \texttt{card\_galois\_reps\_eq\_totient}]
$\#\,\Hom(G,\C^\times) = \varphi(n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Transport the previous count across the isomorphism of Theorem~\ref{thm:gl1}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Prime count; \texttt{card\_galois\_reps\_prime}]
For prime $p$, $\#\,\Hom(G,\C^\times) = p-1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Specialize to $n=p$ and use $\varphi(p)=p-1$.
\end{proof}

\section{Self-duality of the Galois group}

\begin{theorem}[Pontryagin self-duality; \texttt{galoisRepsEquivGalois}]\label{thm:selfdual}
There is a (non-canonical) group isomorphism $\Hom(G,\C^\times) \xrightarrow{\ \cong\ } G$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A finite abelian group $A$ with values in a field with enough roots of unity satisfies $\Hom(A,\C^\times) \cong A$ (non-canonically). Set $U=(\Z/n\Z)^\times$ and compose
\[
\Hom(G,\C^\times) \xrightarrow{\mathrm{precomp}(\artin^{-1})} \Hom(U,\C^\times) \xrightarrow{\text{duality}} U \xrightarrow{\artin^{-1}} G.
\]
\end{proof}

The isomorphism $G \cong \dual{G}$ is non-canonical; only the double dual is canonically $G$.

\begin{theorem}[Galois group order; \texttt{card\_galois\_group\_eq\_totient}]
$\#G = \varphi(n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Transport across $\artin$: $\#G = \#(\Z/n\Z)^\times = \varphi(n)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cardinality shadow of self-duality; \texttt{card\_galois\_reps\_eq\_card\_galois}]
$\#\,\Hom(G,\C^\times) = \#G$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Read off the isomorphism of Theorem~\ref{thm:selfdual}, not by recomputing both sides.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Prime order; \texttt{card\_galois\_group\_prime}]
For prime $p$, $\#G = p-1$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Cyclicity at primes}

\begin{theorem}[Cyclicity; \texttt{galois\_cyclic\_prime}]\label{thm:cyclic}
For prime $p$, $G = \Gal(\Q(\zeta_p)/\Q)$ is cyclic.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The multiplicative group $(\Z/p\Z)^\times$ of the field $\F_p$ is cyclic of order $p-1$. The surjective homomorphism $\artin^{-1}\colon (\Z/p\Z)^\times \to G$ carries cyclicity to $G$. A generator is the image of a primitive root mod $p$.
\end{proof}

\section{The quadratic stratum}

\begin{definition}[Square roots of unity]
For a group $A$, set $\Sqone(A) = \{x \in A : x^2 = 1\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Transport of the quadratic count; \texttt{card\_sq\_eq\_one\_congr}]\label{lem:sqcongr}
If $e\colon A \xrightarrow{\cong} B$ is a group isomorphism then $\#\Sqone(A) = \#\Sqone(B)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$x^2=1 \iff e(x)^2=1$, so $e$ restricts to a bijection $\Sqone(A)\to\Sqone(B)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Square roots of unity mod an odd prime; \texttt{card\_units\_sq\_eq\_one\_prime}]\label{lem:sqp}
For an odd prime $p$, $\#\{x \in (\Z/p\Z)^\times : x^2=1\} = 2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
In the field $\F_p$, $x^2=1$ factors as $(x-1)(x+1)=0$, with roots $\pm 1$, distinct iff $p \neq 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quadratic Dirichlet characters; \texttt{card\_quadratic\_dirichlet\_prime}]
For an odd prime $p$, there are exactly two Dirichlet characters $\chi$ mod $p$ with $\chi^2=1$: the trivial character and the Legendre symbol $\left(\tfrac{\cdot}{p}\right)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The character group is self-dual to $(\Z/p\Z)^\times$; transport Lemma~\ref{lem:sqp} via Lemma~\ref{lem:sqcongr}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quadratic Galois representations; \texttt{card\_quadratic\_galois\_reps\_prime}]
For an odd prime $p$, there are exactly two one-dimensional representations $\rho$ of $\Gal(\Q(\zeta_p)/\Q)$ with $\rho^2=1$: the trivial one and the quadratic character cutting out the unique quadratic subfield $\Q(\sqrt{p^*})$, $p^* = (-1)^{(p-1)/2}p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Transport the count $2$ across the GL(1) correspondence (Theorem~\ref{thm:gl1}) via Lemma~\ref{lem:sqcongr}.
\end{proof}

\section{Local--global factorization}

\begin{lemma}[Universal property of products; \texttt{homProdEquiv}]
For commutative $A,B,M$, $\Hom(A\times B, M) \cong \Hom(A,M)\times\Hom(B,M)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[CRT on units; \texttt{unitsCRT}]
For coprime $m,k$, $(\Z/mk\Z)^\times \cong (\Z/m\Z)^\times \times (\Z/k\Z)^\times$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Hecke factorization; \texttt{heckeFactorization}]\label{thm:hecke}
For coprime $m,k$, $\mathrm{Dir}(\C, mk) \cong \mathrm{Dir}(\C, m) \times \mathrm{Dir}(\C, k)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Compose the unit-group identification, pullback along $\mathrm{unitsCRT}^{-1}$, the product splitting, and re-identification.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Multiplicativity of $\varphi$, structurally; \texttt{card\_dirichlet\_mul}]
For coprime nonzero $m,k$, $\varphi(mk) = \varphi(m)\varphi(k)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Transport cardinality across Theorem~\ref{thm:hecke}.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Artin map evaluation.} Given $\sigma(\zeta_n) = \zeta_n^a$ return $a \bmod n$; inverse $a \mapsto (\zeta_n \mapsto \zeta_n^a)$. Cost $O(\log n)$ per evaluation.

\textbf{Character table.} Compute the cyclic decomposition of $(\Z/n\Z)^\times$ and enumerate all $\varphi(n)$ characters as roots of unity on generators. Cost $O(\varphi(n)^2)$.

\textbf{Quadratic stratum.} Enumerate $x$ with $x^2 \equiv 1$ (for odd $p$, $\{1, p-1\}$); the nontrivial character is the Legendre symbol via Euler's criterion $\left(\tfrac{a}{p}\right) \equiv a^{(p-1)/2}$. Cost $O(p)$.

\section{Numerical illustrations}

For $n=12$: units $\{1,5,7,11\}$, $\#G = \varphi(12) = 4$, $G \cong (\Z/2)^2$ (not cyclic); all four elements square to $1$. For $p=7$: $G$ cyclic of order $6$, generator the residue $3$ ($3,2,6,4,5,1$); quadratic stratum $\{1,6\}$, subfield $\Q(\sqrt{-7})$. For $m=3,k=5$: $\varphi(15)=8=\varphi(3)\varphi(5)$.

\section{Discussion}

Every count is obtained by transport across a genuine isomorphism, never by recomputation, faithful to the Langlands philosophy in which the correspondence is primary and numerical agreements are shadows. The self-duality is honestly non-canonical. Over $\Q$ everything is unconditional, and by Kronecker--Weber the cyclotomic family captures all abelian extensions.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{Order-stratified correspondence.} For prime $p$ and $d \mid p-1$, the number of order-$d$ Dirichlet characters should equal the number of order-$d$ Galois representations, both $\varphi(d)$; both sides are dual to the same cyclic $(\Z/p\Z)^\times$, where a cyclic group of order $m$ has $\varphi(d)$ elements of order $d$ for each $d \mid m$.

\textbf{Tower functoriality.} For $d \mid n$, reduction $(\Z/n\Z)^\times \to (\Z/d\Z)^\times$ should correspond to restriction $\Gal(\Q(\zeta_n)/\Q) \to \Gal(\Q(\zeta_d)/\Q)$, dually inflation of characters --- class field theory functorial in the conductor.

\textbf{Frobenius reciprocity.} For $\ell \nmid n$, the Artin map should send $\Frob_\ell$ to the class of $\ell$; equivalently $\chi(\ell) = \rho(\Frob_\ell)$ at unramified $\ell$, the seed of local--global compatibility in all ranks.

\section{Conclusion}

We have given a complete, self-contained treatment of the cyclotomic GL(1) correspondence over $\Q$: Artin reciprocity, the correspondence as a group isomorphism, structural self-duality, cyclicity at primes, and the quadratic stratum. Each numerical fact descends from a genuine isomorphism.

\end{document}

